reflexivePower -- computes a reflexive power of an ideal in a normal domain
***************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        reflexivePower( n, I )
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, 
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Strategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default value
        IdealStrategy, specify the strategy passed to reflexify
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, 

Description
===========

This function returns the $n$-th reflexive power of $I$.  By definition this is
the reflexification of $I^n$, or in other words, $Hom(Hom(I^n, R), R)$.

+----------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y,z]/ideal(x^2-y*z);|
+----------------------------------+
|i2 : J = ideal(x,y);              |
|                                  |
|o2 : Ideal of R                   |
+----------------------------------+
|i3 : reflexivePower(5, J)         |
|                                  |
|             3     2              |
|o3 = ideal (y , x*y )             |
|                                  |
|o3 : Ideal of R                   |
+----------------------------------+
|i4 : reflexivePower(6, J)         |
|                                  |
|            3                     |
|o4 = ideal y                      |
|                                  |
|o4 : Ideal of R                   |
+----------------------------------+

This function is typically much faster than reflexifying $I^n$ however.  We can
obtain this speedup, because in a normal domain, the reflexification of $I^n$
is the same as the reflexification of the ideal generated by the $n$-th powers
of the generators of $I$.  Consider the example of a cone over a point on an
elliptic curve.

+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : R = QQ[x,y,z]/ideal(-y^2*z +x^3 + x^2*z + x*z^2+z^3);|
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : I = ideal(x-z,y-2*z);                                |
|                                                          |
|o6 : Ideal of R                                           |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : time J20a = reflexivePower(20, I);                   |
| -- used 0.0358873s (cpu); 0.0358877s (thread); 0s (gc)   |
|                                                          |
|o7 : Ideal of R                                           |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : I20 = I^20;                                          |
|                                                          |
|o8 : Ideal of R                                           |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : time J20b = reflexify(I20);                          |
| -- used 0.165081s (cpu); 0.16508s (thread); 0s (gc)      |
|                                                          |
|o9 : Ideal of R                                           |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : J20a == J20b                                        |
|                                                          |
|o10 = true                                                |
+----------------------------------------------------------+

This passes the Strategy option to a reflexify call.  Valid options are
IdealStrategy and ModuleStrategy.

+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : R = QQ[x,y,z]/ideal(-y^2*z +x^3 + x^2*z + x*z^2+z^3);     |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : I = ideal(x-z,y-2*z);                                     |
|                                                                |
|o12 : Ideal of R                                                |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : time J1 = reflexivePower(20, I, Strategy=>IdealStrategy); |
| -- used 0.112125s (cpu); 0.0670853s (thread); 0s (gc)          |
|                                                                |
|o13 : Ideal of R                                                |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : time J2 = reflexivePower(20, I, Strategy=>ModuleStrategy);|
| -- used 0.0647614s (cpu); 0.0647651s (thread); 0s (gc)         |
|                                                                |
|o14 : Ideal of R                                                |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : J1 == J2                                                  |
|                                                                |
|o15 = true                                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note reflexify: reflexify, -- calculate the double dual of an ideal or
    module Hom(Hom(M, R), R)
  * isReflexive(Ideal) (missing documentation)

Ways to use reflexivePower:
===========================

  * "reflexivePower(ZZ,Ideal)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note reflexivePower: reflexivePower, is a *note method function
with options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

