torsionSubmodule -- create the torsion submodule of a module
************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        torsionSubmodule( M1 )
  * Inputs:
      * M1, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Strategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default value
        NoStrategy, specify a strategy for the internal call to reflexify
      * KnownDomain => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, assume the ambient ring is a domain for the internal call
        to reflexify
  * Outputs:
      * a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, 

Description
===========

Finds the torsion submodule of a module.  It does this by computing the kernel
of the map from M1 to its reflexification.  The options Strategy and
KnownDomain are passed along when the function reflexify is called.

+-----------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];            |
+-----------------------------+
|i2 : m = ideal(x,y);         |
|                             |
|o2 : Ideal of R              |
+-----------------------------+
|i3 : M = (R^1/m) ++ R^1      |
|                             |
|o3 = cokernel | x y |        |
|              | 0 0 |        |
|                             |
|                            2|
|o3 : R-module, quotient of R |
+-----------------------------+
|i4 : prune torsionSubmodule M|
|                             |
|o4 = cokernel | y x |        |
|                             |
|                            1|
|o4 : R-module, quotient of R |
+-----------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note reflexify: reflexify, -- calculate the double dual of an ideal or
    module Hom(Hom(M, R), R)

